WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.825 --> 00:00:03.325
[BEEPS]

00:00:06.763 --> 00:00:08.180
PRESENTER: In this
video, we'll be

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.550
showing you the
latest in RStudio 1.4

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.260
and how these
features can benefit

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:16.250
you and your team for credible,
agile, and durable data

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:19.330
science results.

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:22.460
The visual editor is one of
the more exciting new options,

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:26.280
making working with code in
R Markdown easier than ever.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:30.210
This new interface allows you to
compose actual R Markdown using

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.460
the familiar visual interface.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.270
With it, you have easy
access to format commands,

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:41.640
inserting images, tables, just
to name a few of the options

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.110
available in the visual pane.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:49.100
Continuing our theme for
ease of use when authoring,

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.210
you also have the ability
to find and insert

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:54.860
citations from multiple
sources, including integration

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:59.650
with Zetero, a popular free and
open source reference manager.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:01.810
This not only makes
a citation for you,

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:04.000
but it also creates
your bibliography,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.682
reducing the time spent in busy
work while providing research.

00:01:08.682 --> 00:01:10.890
Another thing we spent quite
a bit of time working on

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:14.878
in RStudio 1.4 is
Python, which we focused

00:01:14.878 --> 00:01:16.170
more on in our latest releases.

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:21.280
The largest feature
now available

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.250
is support for Python
in the environment pane.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:26.780
This allows you
to switch directly

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.150
between R mode and
Python mode in that

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:33.170
pane to look at data in
different environments in R

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.240
or immediately change
the view to look at data

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.970
in different modules in Python.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:41.390
We can browse these
Python modules in the pane

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:44.720
or open it to view
directly in our workspace.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.700
This allows you to
interact with data

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.550
in Python in much the
same way that you interact

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:53.660
with data in R. While
RStudio is powered by R

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:56.030
and will always
be an R-first IDE,

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:58.310
we will continue to work
to improve the ability

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:01.190
to use Python alongside
it, allowing teams

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:04.790
to mix these languages
to suit their needs.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:06.850
In addition, Python
environment selections

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:10.870
are now available, allowing you
to select the version of Python

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:14.080
you would like to use or
switch between at any moment

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.650
or use in each project.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.870
This is all powered by
the reticulate package,

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:24.730
which enables these Python-based
features in RStudio 1.4.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:27.190
Another new feature that
many users are excited about

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:29.560
are rainbow
parentheses, which helps

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.300
you to more easily
view and decipher

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.520
different sections of your code.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.040
This ultimately aids in
following deeply nested

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:39.040
constructs, allowing you to
view the different levels

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.660
and find the connected pairs.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.570
In addition, you can now
use any model space font

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:48.060
you like to customize
your coding experience,

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.940
allowing you to tailor the
environment to your preference.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.430
Mainly designed for
widescreen monitors,

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:57.340
we also provide the ability to
have multiple source columns

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:00.260
in the RStudio pane layout.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:01.640
This allows you
to take advantage

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:03.470
of any space you may
have while avoiding

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:07.710
the opening of endless
windows on your screen.

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:11.340
Also new in RStudio 1.4
is the command palette,

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.200
which allows you to
invoke any RStudio

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.260
command with just a few
keystrokes, such as opening

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.480
a new R Markdown document.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.090
This also allows you to
very easily find and learn

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.240
custom shortcuts that you
can use in your everyday work

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.370
and increase your efficiency.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:29.560
One key feature that
is available in both

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:32.980
the open source and commercial
server version of the RStudio

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:36.510
IDE is the ability to
customize where your data

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:41.340
and configuration are stored for
both the users and the servers.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:44.070
This opens up workflows
for mounting configuration

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:47.850
within containers, helping to
solve the long-standing problem

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:50.280
of users filling up
their home directories

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:54.610
and guiding content to where
it should appropriately go.

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:56.643
Moving from the open
source, let's also

00:03:56.643 --> 00:03:58.060
walk through the
new features that

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:02.970
are now available with the
Pro-edition of RStudio 1.4.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.130
Undoubtedly the most
requested and important

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:06.780
of our recent additions
is the ability

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:11.520
to authenticate RStudio
Server Pro now using SAML.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.030
This option along with open
IDE enables single sign-on

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:18.209
for users and systems with
standardization that provides

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:21.720
fast yet reliable security.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.480
In addition to load balancing
with a local launcher,

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:27.810
project sharing with a
launcher is also now available.

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.820
Previously, both
these popular features

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.647
were not available to
use at the same time.

00:04:31.647 --> 00:04:33.480
But now, even when
you've [? launcherized ?]

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:36.180
an environment, you can
still share your projects

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:37.710
with others.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.290
While it still requires
some setup for permissions,

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:43.080
these are both big features with
the commercial server version

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:46.200
of the RStudio IDE that
are now available to use

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.490
at the same time.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:51.940
Of note for those who need
to request a GPU with SLURM,

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:57.270
this is now possible with
custom S batch parameters.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:04:59.880
Another nice new feature for
the commercial server version

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.280
of the RStudio
IDE is the ability

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.130
to run Visual Studio
Code sessions.

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:07.500
This is currently in beta
and is great for those

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:10.710
who want to run longer form
Python programs while working

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.800
with other teams and projects
within the RStudio Server

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:14.700
environment.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.150
[BEEPS]

